}

# Write a Rocdown page

Start with ordinary Markdown. Add `@page` when you need a title, layout,
or a stable route.

````rocdown
@page {
    meta: {
        title: "Hello from Rocdown",
        description: "A small Markdown-first page",
    },
}

# Hello from Rocdown

This paragraph is ordinary Markdown.
````

Rocdown can derive routes from file paths, so documentation sites usually
omit `route`. Use an explicit route when an existing URL must stay
stable.

Fenced code is always display-only, even if the language is `roc`,
`rocci`, or `rocdown`. A documentation example does not become build-time
or runtime code.

## Preview

```sh
rocdown view hello.rocdown
```

Standalone preview uses the default reading shell. Pages with H2 or H3
headings get an “On this page” navigator. Site builds keep a separate
outline inside documentation chrome.

## Links that work on a site

On a Rocdown site, prefer stable absolute routes:

```markdown
Read the [Rocdown language](/docs/rocdown/language/).
```

Sibling preview also understands `[[Page]]` and `[label](Page.rocdown)`.
Do not point user docs at a knowledge bundle.

On a catalog site, `index.rocdown` is the section landing for its
directory. Nested directories and Overview rows are in
[site configuration](/docs/rocdown/sites/) and the
[contributor checklist](/docs/reference/contributor/checklist/).

For asides, figures, and tabs, see
[Article blocks](/docs/rocdown/blocks/).

## Hydrate a value into Markdown

This heading is optional. `@{expr}` splices a Roc `Str` from `@roc` into
prose. The page becomes `hydrate`. Do not use it on static `docs/`
catalog pages (`RD2303`).

````rocdown
@roc {
published = "2026-08-16"
}

Published @{published}.
````

Live pages that run handlers are
[hybrid publish](/docs/rocdown/hybrid/).

